.TH SNYPR 1 "2026" "snypr" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
snypr \- screenshot, annotation, and live-drawing tool for Hyprland
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B snypr
[\fIGLOBAL OPTIONS\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fICOMMAND OPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B snypr
is a GTK4-based screenshot, annotation, and live-drawing tool targeting
.BR Hyprland (1)
and other wlroots-based compositors. It captures pixels via the
\fIzwlr_screencopy_manager_v1\fR Wayland protocol, renders selectors and overlays through
\fIgtk4-layer-shell\fR, and draws annotations on a GTK4 canvas.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
Use \fIPATH\fR instead of \fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/snypr/config.toml\fR. Applies to every
subcommand, and is an error if \fIPATH\fR does not exist. Ignored with
\fB\-\-via-daemon\fR, where the request runs in the daemon's process against the
configuration that daemon started with.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Increase log verbosity (repeatable). \fB\-v\fR selects debug, \fB\-vv\fR trace; either
overrides \fBRUST_LOG\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-lang " " \fIBCP47\fR
Override the active language as a BCP-47 tag (\fIfr\fR, \fIen-US\fR, ...). Falls back to
the \fBlanguage\fR field in \fIconfig.toml\fR, then \fBLC_ALL\fR / \fBLC_MESSAGES\fR /
\fBLANG\fR, then English.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS screenshot
Capture pixels and pipe them to one or more sinks. With \fB\-\-edit\fR the captured image
is handed to the in-place annotation overlay before reaching the sinks.
.TP
.BR \-\-full
Stitch the bounding box covering every output into a single image.
.TP
.BR \-\-per\-output
Produce one file per output. \fB{output}\fR is inserted into the filename template
automatically when the template does not already contain it, so the files cannot collapse
onto one another. Incompatible with \fB\-\-edit\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-focused
Capture the currently focused monitor, queried over Hyprland's IPC.
.TP
.BR \-\-window
Capture the currently active window, queried over Hyprland's IPC.
.TP
.BR \-\-output " " \fINAME\fR
Capture a single output (\fIDP-1\fR, \fIeDP-1\fR, ...).
.TP
.BR \-\-region " " \fIX,Y,WxH\fR
Capture an explicit logical-pixel region.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Open the selector overlay and let the user draw a region.
.TP
.BR \-\-edit
Open the in-place annotation overlay on the captured image before writing to sinks. The
overlay opens one layer-shell window per intersecting monitor so the captured pixels are
edited where they live on the desktop. Incompatible with \fB\-\-per\-output\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-to " " \fISINK\fR
Add an output sink. May be passed multiple times. Recognised forms:
\fBfile\fR (write to the configured directory), \fBfile=\fIPATH\fR (write to \fIPATH\fR),
\fBclipboard\fR (write to the Wayland clipboard via \fIwl-clipboard-rs\fR; no
\fIwl-copy\fR binary is spawned),
\fBclipboard=\fIKIND\fR where \fIKIND\fR is \fBregular\fR, \fBprimary\fR, or \fBboth\fR
(per-sink override of \fB\-\-clipboard\-type\fR).
When running outside the daemon, the clipboard sink forks a short detached child
process that keeps serving the selection after \fBsnypr\fR exits so paste
keeps working until another client overtakes the selection.
In the interactive selector, the annotation editor, and the draw overlay, the resolved
sinks only seed the toolbar's output-destination switcher: the user can cycle it (button
click or \fBCtrl+O\fR) between file, clipboard, and both before capturing or saving. An explicit \fIPATH\fR or \fI=KIND\fR is
remembered across the cycle.
.TP
.BR \-\-clipboard\-type " " \fIKIND\fR
Default clipboard selection (\fBregular\fR, \fBprimary\fR, or \fBboth\fR) used for every
\fB\-\-to clipboard\fR sink that does not carry an explicit \fI=KIND\fR suffix.
Falls back to \fB[clipboard].default_kind\fR in \fIconfig.toml\fR, then to \fBregular\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-cursor
Include the hardware cursor in the capture. Turns on \fB[capture].cursor\fR for this
invocation; since the flag is a bare boolean it can only enable, so use the selector's
cursor toggle to leave the pointer out when \fBcursor = true\fR is configured.
.TP
.BR \-\-delay " " \fISECONDS\fR
Wait \fISECONDS\fR whole seconds before capturing. \fB0\fR is the same as omitting the
flag. The UI countdown only operates on integer seconds, so the CLI, config, and IPC
representations all match.
.TP
.BR \-\-via-daemon
Forward the request to a running \fBsnypr daemon\fR over its Unix socket instead of
executing locally.
.SS draw
Launch the live draw-on-screen overlay. Tool keys: \fBR\fR rectangle, \fBO\fR ellipse,
\fBA\fR arrow, \fBL\fR line (no arrowhead), \fBH\fR highlight, \fBF\fR freehand, \fBN\fR
numbered marker, \fBT\fR text, \fBB\fR blur, \fBX\fR redact (press the active tool's key
again, or click its button, to deactivate it). Other keys: \fBCtrl+Z\fR undo, \fBCtrl+L\fR
clear all layers, \fBCtrl+S\fR / \fBEnter\fR save via the zone selector, \fBCtrl+O\fR cycle
the output destination (file \(-> clipboard \(-> both), \fBP\fR toggle
pointer passthrough, \fBEsc\fR quit. \fBCrop\fR is editor-only and is not available in the
live overlay; \fBBlur\fR is available, and grabs the underlying desktop the first time it
is used. With no tool active the overlay is in \fBSelect\fR mode:
click an existing shape to pick it, drag its body to move it, drag a handle to resize it
(an Arrow/Line endpoint, a numbered marker's radius, or — for text — corner handles scale
the font while side handles set a wrap width), double-click text to re-edit it, press
\fBDelete\fR / \fBBackspace\fR to remove it or \fBEsc\fR to deselect, and arrow keys to
nudge (\fBShift\fR for a larger step). After committing a shape the overlay returns to
Select mode automatically.
.TP
.BR \-\-passthrough
Open the overlay with pointer passthrough already enabled, so clicks fall through to the
windows underneath until \fBP\fR (or \fB\-\-toggle-passthrough\fR) turns it off.
.TP
.BR \-\-via-daemon
Toggle a daemon-managed overlay instead of spawning a new process.
.TP
.BR \-\-toggle-passthrough
Flip pointer passthrough on the running overlay from a global keybind. Requires
\fB\-\-via-daemon\fR.
.SS daemon
Run the IPC server on \fI$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/snypr.sock\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-socket " " \fIPATH\fR
Listen on \fIPATH\fR instead of \fI$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/snypr.sock\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-systray
Also register a StatusNotifierItem with Screenshot / Annotate / Draw / Quit menu items.
.SS doctor
Print a copy-pasteable Markdown diagnostic report covering version, runtime
environment, parsed configuration, and live capability probes (Hyprland IPC,
wlr-screencopy outputs, daemon socket \fBPing\fR). All headings are level 3 so the
output drops cleanly under a user-supplied level-2 heading. Always exits with status
\fB0\fR; failed probes surface as \fBWARN\fR / \fBFAIL\fR lines in the report.
.SH MODES
The \fBscreenshot\fR subcommand surfaces two interactive UI modes, neither of which is a
top-level subcommand.
.SS Selector
Shown by \fBscreenshot\fR without an explicit selection flag (or with
\fB\-\-interactive\fR). A single floating toolbar \(em shown on the focused monitor and
following keyboard focus as it changes \(em offers Full / Screen / Window / Region modes,
a cursor toggle, a delay spinner, an output-destination switcher (\fBCtrl+O\fR cycles
file \(-> clipboard \(-> both), and a single action button:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCapture\fR (\fBEnter\fR) commits the selection and writes the result to sinks. Holding
\fBShift\fR while clicking (or pressing \fBShift+Enter\fR / \fBShift+KP_Enter\fR) instead
captures and then opens the in-place annotation editor before writing. The button's icon
and tooltip swap live while Shift is held, and the output-destination switcher greys out
for as long as it is \(em the editor that opens carries its own, seeded with whatever the
selector's switcher was set to.
.RE
The Shift modifier ORs into the CLI \fB\-\-edit\fR flag: Shift-click on a plain
\fBscreenshot\fR opens the editor, and \fB\-\-edit\fR still opens the editor even on a
plain Capture click. Keyboard: \fB1\fR/\fB2\fR/\fB3\fR/\fB4\fR switch modes, \fBEsc\fR
cancels.
.SS Editor (in-place annotation overlay)
Opened by \fBscreenshot \-\-edit\fR or by a Shift-click / \fBShift+Enter\fR on the
selector's Capture button. Tool keys: \fBR\fR rectangle, \fBO\fR ellipse, \fBA\fR arrow,
\fBL\fR line, \fBH\fR highlight, \fBF\fR freehand, \fBN\fR numbered marker, \fBT\fR text,
\fBB\fR blur, \fBX\fR redact, \fBC\fR crop (press the active tool's key again, or click its
button, to deactivate it). Other keys: \fBCtrl+Z\fR undo, \fBCtrl+S\fR / \fBEnter\fR save,
\fBCtrl+O\fR cycle the output destination (file \(-> clipboard \(-> both), \fBEsc\fR quit.
With no tool active the editor is in \fBSelect\fR mode and edits existing
shapes: click to select, drag the body to move, drag a handle to resize (box shapes get
eight handles; Arrow/Line expose their endpoints; a numbered marker exposes a radius grip;
text corner handles scale the font while side handles set a wrap width), double-click text
to re-edit it in place, \fBDelete\fR / \fBBackspace\fR to remove the selection, \fBEsc\fR
to deselect, and arrow keys to nudge (\fBShift\fR for a larger step). After committing a
shape the editor returns to Select mode automatically. A color picker (with alpha) and a
stroke-style picker (Solid / Dashed / Dotted) sit next to the tool buttons; each tool
remembers its own color and style, and the pickers also act on the selected shape (or the
text being edited). The pickers are disabled for tools with hardcoded appearance
(\fBBlur\fR, \fBCrop\fR, \fBRedact\fR) and when nothing relevant is selected. The same
pickers are available in the live \fBdraw\fR overlay.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B snypr
reads \fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/snypr/config.toml\fR. Every field is optional; see the
sample in the project README. The top-level \fBlanguage\fR field sets the UI language as
a BCP-47 tag and is overridden by \fB\-\-lang\fR. Filename template tokens: \fB{ts}\fR,
\fB{date}\fR, \fB{time}\fR, \fB{output}\fR, \fB{selection}\fR. The \fB[ui.selector]\fR
table
overrides the selector chrome colors (\fIoutline\fR, \fIoutline_hover\fR,
\fIlabel\fR, \fIdim_strong\fR,
\fIdim_full\fR, \fIdim_light\fR, \fIcountdown_fg\fR, \fIcountdown_bg\fR) as
\fB#RRGGBB\fR or \fB#RRGGBBAA\fR hex strings. The \fB[capture]\fR table accepts
\fIcursor\fR (include the pointer by default; see \fB\-\-cursor\fR) and
\fIinitial_mode\fR (\fBfull\fR / \fBscreen\fR / \fBwindow\fR / \fBregion\fR; default
\fBscreen\fR) to pre-select a mode button in the interactive selector. The
\fB[annotate.colors]\fR table sets the default color of each annotation tool
(\fIrect\fR, \fIellipse\fR, \fIarrow\fR, \fIline\fR, \fIfreehand\fR, \fIhighlight\fR,
\fInumber\fR, \fItext\fR) as \fB#RRGGBB\fR or \fB#RRGGBBAA\fR hex strings; the
toolbar color picker still lets the user change colors at runtime.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SNYPR_CONFIG
Alternative configuration file path. Equivalent to \fB\-\-config\fR.
.TP
.B SNYPR_LANG
UI language as a BCP-47 tag. Equivalent to \fB\-\-lang\fR.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Standard \fItracing-subscriber\fR filter. Overridden by \fB\-v\fR / \fB\-vv\fR.
.TP
.B SNYPR_CAPTURE_GRACE_MS
Grace period, in milliseconds, between dismissing the selector surface and grabbing
pixels. Defaults to \fB30\fR. Raise it on compositors whose surface teardown is slow
enough that the selector chrome leaks into the capture.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/snypr/config.toml
User configuration.
.TP
.I $XDG_PICTURES_DIR/Screenshots/
Default save directory.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/snypr.sock
Daemon control socket.
.TP
.I $PREFIX/share/applications/noirbizar.re.Snypr.desktop
Standalone launcher (Screenshot/Draw actions).
.TP
.I $PREFIX/share/applications/noirbizar.re.Snypr.Daemon.desktop
Launcher for the daemon + systray (\fBsnypr daemon \-\-systray\fR).
.TP
.I $PREFIX/share/icons/hicolor/<size>/apps/noirbizar.re.Snypr.png
Application icon, resolved through the hicolor theme. Shipped sizes:
16, 32, 48, 64, 128, 256, 512.
.TP
.I $PREFIX/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/apps/noirbizar.re.Snypr.svg
Scalable application icon, preferred by consumers that render at arbitrary sizes.
